---
/**
 * /about — the person behind burningsuit.
 *
 * Copy is the SIGNED deck at design/copy/about.md (v5, signed 2026-06-17, with
 * Alan's inline edits). Voice: warm outward, light about himself, "for everyone
 * else" as the spine, spoken cadence. Structure mirrors /colophon (prose
 * chapters on the aubergine ground): page-hero → thread → chapters → thread-end.
 *
 * PHOTOS PENDING: the "where burningsuit comes from" and "and the dog" sections
 * have comment hooks where real photos drop in later (Stuart & Alison; Zelda).
 * They're text-only for now so the build stays green and snapshots deterministic
 * — add <Picture> + regenerate the visual baselines when the images land.
 *
 * OPEN COPY ITEM: DataKind line not yet placed (Alan deciding the angle).
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const contactSubject = "Hello";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — burningsuit: Power BI, Fabric & AI for everyone else"
  description="I'm Alan Harman-Box. burningsuit helps Power BI and Fabric teams use AI well — built for everyone else, the people who just want to get their job done, not the ones already knee-deep in agents."
  contactSubject={contactSubject}
>
  <Fragment slot="footer-invitation"
    >If any of this sounds like the kind of thing you're dealing with, I'd
    genuinely like to hear about it</Fragment
  >

  <section class="page-hero" data-ground="aubergine">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker meta enter">about</p>
      <h1 class="enter appear-300">power bi and ai — for everyone <em>else</em>.</h1>
      <p class="lead enter appear-450">
        I'm Alan, and these days burningsuit is me. There's a lot of noise about
        AI and Power BI at the moment, and most of it feels aimed at the people
        who are already knee-deep in 'agents'. I'm interested in everyone else —
        the people who just want to get their job done well and would rather not
        have to turn into a nerd to do it. Which is fine by me — I'm more than
        happy to be the nerd in the room, so you don't have to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <section class="chapter" data-ground="aubergine" aria-labelledby="capable">
    <div class="wrap what">
      <h2 class="label kicker meta" id="capable" data-reveal>i start from what people can do</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          I've got a fairly stubborn belief that, with the right instruction and
          a bit of encouragement, almost anyone can learn almost anything — so I
          won't turn up assuming a team's behind, or that the people are the
          problem. Most of the teams I meet are more capable than they've been
          given credit for; they're just busy with the day job, a bit wary of
          the latest thing, and short on someone to think it through with.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          And I can go as deep into the technical side of things as you want me
          to, but honestly that's never really the point for me. The point is
          your team being able to do what they need to do, and actually feeling
          good while they're doing it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" data-ground="aubergine" aria-labelledby="human">
    <div class="wrap what">
      <h2 class="label kicker meta" id="human" data-reveal>the part that isn't really technical</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          The nuts and bolts of Power BI I can do, and I've done it a long time.
          But that's honestly not usually the thing that makes the difference.
          It's much more about whether people who are busy, and a bit sceptical,
          actually feel like having a go — and whether, by the end, your team
          reckons I get how they work, rather than someone turning up to tell
          them they've been doing it wrong. That's really the part I care about
          getting right.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          I've spent about five years working with organisations getting to
          grips with Power BI and Fabric, and before that a decade or so in
          software delivery — large systems, some of it for government. I also
          give a talk at a few user groups, called "we're all middle management
          now", about being responsible for work you've handed off to something
          that can't really be held responsible for it. The thing that's stuck
          with me through all of it is that these projects mostly succeed or
          fail on the people side, well before the technology really comes into
          it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" data-ground="aubergine" aria-labelledby="origin">
    <div class="wrap what">
      <h2 class="label kicker meta" id="origin" data-reveal>where burningsuit comes from</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          burningsuit was my parents' Power BI training business. They handed it
          over last year and they're properly retired now, so these days it's
          me. I've taken it somewhere a bit different from where it started —
          these days it's more about advising and designing alongside teams than
          running courses — but the reason it exists hasn't really changed.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          As my mum said, burningsuit was for "everyone else" — normal people,
          basically. Not the nerds, not the people who are always online
          performing their work — just someone trying to get a job done. And
          that's still the focus.
        </p>
        {/* photo: Stuart & Alison — drop in when supplied */}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" data-ground="aubergine" aria-labelledby="dog">
    <div class="wrap what">
      <h2 class="label kicker meta" id="dog" data-reveal>and the dog</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          Last thing: this is Zelda, who I'm told is Chief Morale Officer. She's
          no use at all with the DAX, but she's very good at making me get up and
          go outside now and then, which honestly does more for the work than
          you'd think.
        </p>
        {/* photo: Zelda — drop in when supplied */}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread thread-end" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="talk-spacer" data-ground="aubergine"></div>
</BaseLayout>
